# NOTES
# 1. WebSearch tool budget was exhausted (200/200 session calls used by earlier subagents) before the third research pass began, forcing all three verification agents onto WebFetch-only against known Wikipedia URLs. This materially limited coverage of rpv_manufacturer (world-nuclear.org France profile and IAEA PRIS reactor-detail pages, both approved sources, were never actually reached) and of architect_engineer for the P4/P'4 plants. Recommend a follow-up pass on this batch once search budget resets, targeted specifically at rpv_manufacturer sourcing.
# 2. IAEA PRIS reactor-detail pages (pris.iaea.org) could not be retrieved at all: they render via ASP.NET __doPostBack JavaScript (WebFetch only sees static HTML) and one agent got 403 Forbidden. This is a structural gap, not a per-reactor finding — PRIS should be considered unusable via WebFetch for this whole database unless a JS-capable fetch path is added.
# 3. The single confirmed anachronism the task predicted: Flamanville unit 3 (EPR) has rpv_manufacturer WRONG as "Creusot-Loire" (defunct since 1984) — the real forge was Areva NP/Framatome's Creusot Forge (Le Creusot), the same facility at the center of the 2015 carbon-segregation vessel scandal. Units 1 and 2 at the same plant (P4 palier, 1980s build) were NOT confirmed to use Creusot-Loire either, but are chronologically plausible and were left UNVERIFIABLE rather than corrected.
# 4. Contrary to the "Creusot-Loire is anachronistic for later builds" hypothesis stated in the task brief, no source located for ANY of the mid-1980s to early-1990s P4/P'4 units (Flamanville 1&2, Golfech, Nogent, Paluel, Penly, St. Alban) explicitly names "Creusot-Loire" as their RPV forge — nor does any source explicitly name "Framatome" as the vessel forge specifically (as opposed to the NSSS/reactor system generally). French Wikipedia collapses forge-level detail under the generic "Framatome" vendor label. This means the original heuristic's "Creusot-Loire" value for the entire 1300MW fleet is likely fabricated/unsourced rather than merely anachronistic, but it could not be affirmatively disproven per-unit with available sources. Golfech unit 2 (construction began Oct 1984, essentially the exact month of the Creusot-Loire bankruptcy) is the single highest-risk candidate for a real split from unit 1 if better sourcing is found.
# 5. Known "US-batch bug" pattern (NSSS vendor listed as constructor when the real builder was a construction firm or utility) shows up here in reverse and adjacent forms: G2 and G3 (Marcoule, UNGG) had "constructor=EDF" but were actually CEA-led plutonium-production reactors with EDF as a junior/power-offtake partner — corrected to CEA. Their turbine supplier was Rateau, not Alstom (Alstom/CGE did not yet dominate the market at the 1950s construction date). Phenix's turbine supplier was CEM (Compagnie Electro-Mecanique), not Alstom. Super-Phenix's architect_engineer was NERSA (a dedicated EDF/ENEL/SBK joint venture), not EDF alone, and its turbine supplier was Ansaldo Energia (Italian, reflecting ENEL's stake), not Alstom. The heuristic's blanket "Alstom = French turbine supplier" and "EDF = French architect-engineer" assumptions break down completely for the non-standard CEA-era and fast-breeder reactors.
# 6. St. Laurent A (units 1, 2 — UNGG gas-cooled, retired) is almost entirely UNVERIFIABLE: available Wikipedia sources describe operator status but do not clearly separate constructor/AE/turbine-supplier roles for the UNGG-era A units (as distinct from the later PWR B units at the same site, where Alstom is well confirmed). This is a genuine sourcing gap, not a corrected value — do not backfill from the B-unit pattern.
# 7. Phenix unit 1 constructor is contested rather than cleanly confirmed/wrong: fr.wikipedia describes an integrated CEA/EDF/GAAA build team, where GAAA (Groupement Atomique Alsacienne-Atlantique) was the industrial-build consortium and predecessor of Novatome. The database's "CEA" value is defensible as the program-owner/lead-designer convention, but if this database elsewhere uses "industrial constructor" as its convention (as with Super-Phenix = Novatome), Phenix may need to be recorded as GAAA instead for consistency. Flagged for a human convention call rather than auto-corrected.
# 8. Golfech and Nogent turbine_supplier: sources confirm Alstom as the ORIGINAL-build supplier but note the turbine line was later renamed/transferred to GE France (General Electric) after 2014 (GE's 2014-2015 acquisition of Alstom's power business, later re-spun as Arabelle Solutions/EDF in 2024). Marked CONFIRMED against original-build attribution; if this database's "turbine_supplier" field is meant to reflect current ownership rather than original construction, several fleet-wide entries (Golfech, Nogent, Tricastin, St. Laurent B, Gravelines) would need re-labeling to GE France/Arabelle Solutions instead of Alstom. This is a schema/convention question, not a sourcing gap.
